```markdown
**Title:** Merge near-duplicate wildlife sightings within 50m/10min

**Type:** Feature
**Size:** S
**Epic/Component:** Ingest Pipeline / Deduplication
**Priority:** Medium (assumed — not specified by requester)
**Depends On:** None

---

## Effort Estimate

**Recommended Model Tier:** Standard
**Reasoning:** The change is a single, well-specified pure function with a clear geometric/temporal rule and an existing test module to extend, but it involves enough judgment (boundary handling, merge semantics, which record's fields win) to be more than mechanical Economy-tier work.

---

## User Story

**As a** Fernglen ingest pipeline maintainer
**I want to** merge two sighting records into one when they report the same species within 50 meters and 10 minutes of each other
**So that** duplicate volunteer submissions stop double-counting a single observation

---

## Acceptance Criteria

- [ ] Given two sighting records of the same species reported within 50 meters and within 10 minutes of each other, when `dedupe_nearby_sightings()` processes them, then they are merged into a single record.
- [ ] Given two sighting records of different species (regardless of distance/time proximity), when processed, then they are NOT merged and both records are retained.
- [ ] Given two sighting records of the same species more than 50 meters apart, when processed, then they are NOT merged, even if within 10 minutes of each other.
- [ ] Given two sighting records of the same species more than 10 minutes apart, when processed, then they are NOT merged, even if within 50 meters of each other.
- [ ] Given records exactly at the 50-meter or 10-minute boundary, when processed, then the boundary is treated as inclusive (merge occurs at exactly 50m/10min) and this behavior is asserted by an explicit test case.
- [ ] Given a batch containing more than two candidate duplicates of the same species/location/time cluster, when processed, then the function's merge behavior on 3+-way clusters is defined and tested (either pairwise-chained merge or explicit rejection of >2-way clusters — implementer's choice, but it must be a stated, tested behavior, not undefined).

---

## Technical Notes

**Dependencies:**
- None

**Constraints:**
- None specified

**Files to Create/Modify:**
- `ingest/dedupe.py` — add `dedupe_nearby_sightings()`
- `ingest/test_dedupe.py` — extend existing test module with cases covering the acceptance criteria above

**Architecture Notes:**
- Distance calculation between two sightings needs a defined method (e.g., haversine over lat/lon) — pick and document the existing convention if the ingest pipeline already has one, rather than introducing a second distance utility.
- Merge semantics (which record's fields — timestamp, observer, notes — survive the merge) should be decided explicitly rather than left to whichever record happens to be first in iteration order.

---

